{{also|Phlogiston}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|NL.|phlogiston}}, coined by {{w|Georg Ernst Stahl}} in 1702, from {{der|en|grc|φλογιστόν}}, neuter of {{m|grc|φλογιστός|t=burnt up, inflammable}}, from {{m|grc|φλογίζω|t=to set fire to}}, from {{m|grc|φλόξ|t=flame}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/flə(ʊ)ˈd͡ʒɪstɒn/|/flə(ʊ)ˈd͡ʒɪstən/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-phlogiston.oga|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry|historical}} The [[hypothetical]] [[fiery]] [[principle]] formerly [[assume]]d to be a [[necessary]] [[constituent]] of [[combustible]] [[body#Noun|bodies]] and to be [[give up|given up]] by them in [[burn]]ing.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1810|author=George Wilson, M.D., F.R.D.E.|title=The Life of the Hon<sup>ble</sup> Henry Cavendish|chapter=General Sketch of Cavendish's Scientific Researches and Discoveries|page=39|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/lifeofhonhenryca00wils/page/39 |passage={{...}} air was universally reputed to be a simple or elementary body. It was liable, according to the [[phlogistian]]s, to [[vitiation]], by the addition to it of '''phlogiston'''{{...}} being more or less [[phlogisticated]], according to the degree of its power to support respiration and combustion.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author={{w|Philip Ball}}|title=The Devil's Doctor|publisher=Arrow 2007|page=397|passage=Stahl argued that '''phlogiston''' could explain combustion, a central concern of eighteenth-century chemistry.}}

